body:
I've done some testing on a local Xcode project.
I believe this is a SwiftUI bug/inconsistency.

If I make a new & simple SwiftUI app, it does by default not show the Home Indicator on my iPhone 14 Pro/iOS 26.1, but it does show it on my iPhone X/iOS 16.7.12. (I actually can't force it to show on my iPhone 14 Pro even if I add the `.persistentSystemOverlays(.visible)` modifier anywhere, which I find somewhat odd.)

If I have a `UIViewRepresentable` as is used by Godot: https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/98782b6c8c9cabe0fb7c80bc62640735ecb076d3/drivers/apple_embedded/app.swift#L34, then it does not seem that the hide home indicator setting is respected: https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/98782b6c8c9cabe0fb7c80bc62640735ecb076d3/drivers/apple_embedded/godot_view_controller.mm#L334
This ignoring of the override flag seemingly happens regardless of whether this is done in a Swift/UIKit setup or Obj-C setup.

I did find a non-perfect workaround:
Using the `.persistentSystemOverlays()` SwiftUI modifier, I was able to hide the Home Indicator on my iPhone X.
This is non-perfect, because this modifier still requires iOS 16, which would technically leave users on even older devices with unwanted behavior. I believe this might be ok, since all devices released since 2017 support updating to iOS 16 (https://iosref.com/ios). At the very least, it would be an improvement for any users that are [iOS 16 ... unknown iOS version where hiding Home Indicator works as expected].

I would propose replacing the main view's `body` https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/98782b6c8c9cabe0fb7c80bc62640735ecb076d3/drivers/apple_embedded/app.swift#L53 with something like this:
```
var body: some Scene {
        WindowGroup {
            let viewController = GodotSwiftUIViewController()
                .ignoresSafeArea()
                // UIViewControllerRepresentable does not call viewWillDisappear() nor viewDidDisappear() when
                // backgrounding the app, or closing the app's main window, update the renderer here.
                .onChange(of: scenePhase) { phase in
                    // For some reason UIViewControllerRepresentable is not calling viewWillDisappear()
                    // nor viewDidDisappear when closing the app's main window, call it here so we
                    // stop the renderer.
                    switch phase {
                    case .active:
                        print("GodotSwiftUIViewController scene active")
                        GDTAppDelegateService.viewController?.godotView.startRendering()
                    case .inactive:
                        print("GodotSwiftUIViewController scene inactive")
                        GDTAppDelegateService.viewController?.godotView.stopRendering()
                    case .background:
                        print("GodotSwiftUIViewController scene backgrounded")
                        GDTAppDelegateService.viewController?.godotView.stopRendering()
                    @unknown default:
                        print("unknown default")
                    }
                }

            // For some reason early iOS versions do not respect the `prefersHomeIndicatorAutoHidden` override flag,
            // and the Home Indicator is always displayed.
            // We use the SwiftUI modifier to enforce the override flag we want.
            // Not a perfect solution since there are still some devices that do not have access to this modifier.
            if #available(iOS 16.0, *) {
                let hideHomeIndictor = GLOBAL_GET("display/window/ios/hide_home_indicator")
                viewController.persistentSystemOverlays(hideHomeIndictor ? .hidden : .visible)
            } else {
                viewController
            }
        }
    }
```
I didn't create a PR for this myself for 2 reasons:
1. I wasn't able to get everything set up to compile & export properly locally.
2. I'm not sure if how I'm trying to access the global setting is correct.
